\title{gpuinfo: Lightweight hardware and GPU-backend detection}
\description{
\code{gpuinfo} provides framework-independent, read-only detection of CPU and GPU
hardware and the apparent availability of CUDA, Metal, ROCm, and OpenCL. It
does not require Python, a machine-learning framework, or a vendor SDK.
}
\section{Public API}{

The package intentionally exposes only seven functions. Use
\code{\link[=hardware_info]{hardware_info()}} for the complete structured report, \code{\link[=gpu_sitrep]{gpu_sitrep()}} for a
human-readable diagnostic report, and \code{\link[=has_gpu]{has_gpu()}}, \code{\link[=has_cuda]{has_cuda()}},
\code{\link[=has_metal]{has_metal()}}, \code{\link[=has_rocm]{has_rocm()}}, or \code{\link[=has_opencl]{has_opencl()}} for strict scalar checks.
}

\section{Interpretation}{

A positive backend predicate means that \code{gpuinfo} found the relevant visible
hardware and enough driver/runtime support to regard that backend as usable.
It does not prove that another R package was compiled with that backend. For
example, \code{\link[=has_cuda]{has_cuda()}} can be \code{TRUE} while an installed deep-learning package
is CPU-only.

Detection is limited to devices visible to the current process. Containers,
schedulers, remote sessions, and variables such as \code{CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES}
can intentionally hide physical devices. Missing tools, libraries, drivers,
or permissions produce \code{FALSE}, empty tables, or \code{NA} fields rather than an
error.
}

\section{Detection behavior}{

Probes may load vendor libraries dynamically and may run read-only system
utilities such as \code{nvidia-smi}, \code{system_profiler}, \code{lspci}, \code{rocminfo}, or
\code{clinfo} when available. The package does not modify drivers, environment
variables, device settings, or files, and it does not make network requests.
}

